Project: Global Goals & Local Action Portfolio
Citizenship 9 Collaborative Digital Project
In this project, your group (2–3 people) will move beyond the classroom to act as digital advocates. You will research a pressing issue facing Nova Scotia and Canada, connect it to the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), and build a Google Sites Portfolio to showcase your findings and your "Call to Action." A Google Sites template will be shared on Tuesday.
🏗️ The Structure of Your Portfolio
Your website must have the following pages. Each page needs an Author Byline (e.g., "Page Authored by: [Your Name]") to show who took the lead on that specific content.
1. Home: The Mission
The Hook: A compelling image or headline about your issue.
Mission Statement: A paragraph explaining why your group chose this issue and which Global Goal it aligns with.
The Team: Brief bios of your group members.
2. The Investigation Log
The Global Connection: Identify the specific UN Target (e.g., Target 14.1) that matches your local issue.
Human Impact: A "lived experience" story. What does this issue feel like for a real person in NS?
Consequences of Inaction: A "Future Report" predicting what happens in 20 years if we do nothing.
Author Requirement: At least two different authors must contribute sections here.
3. Canada’s Response (Evaluation)
The Evidence: Find one specific law, project, or statistic showing what the government is currently doing. Based on your research, evaluate if the response is "Effective," "Insufficient," or "Developing."
Authorship: One student should lead this research.
4. The Action Project
Choose Your Path: Embed your final project here:
Option A (The MLA Briefing): Embed your 1-page PDF.
Option B (The Awareness Video): Embed a 60-second PSA video (uploaded to Drive).
A map showing locations of the issue, if we can get around the block on Google Maps (use Bing Maps?)
5. Final Reflection & Sources
All sources cited 
🚀 Detailed First Steps (Do these TODAY)
Step 1: Form & Claim (15 Mins)
Form your group of 2 or 3.
Choose your issue from the Priority List (e.g., Ghost Fishing Gear, Food Security in First Nations).
Teacher Check-in: Once you have an issue, tell the teacher. Issues are first-come, first-served!
Step 2: Begin research (20 Mins)
One person creates a "Blank" doc and names it: [Issue Name] Portfolio - [Group Initials].
IMPORTANT: Click the "Share with others" icon (person with a +) and add your group members and the teacher as Editors.
A Google Sites template will be shared on Tuesday.
Step 3: Assign Roles
Decide who will be the lead for the following (you will still help each other!):
The Researcher: Lead on "Canada's Response" and "Global Connection."
The Storyteller: Lead on "Human Impact" and "Inaction Consequences."
The Tech/Design Lead: Lead on Site layout and the "Action Project" media (Video/Map).
🛠️ Tech Tips for Google Sites (for next week)
Embedding Drive Files: To show a Google Doc (like your Briefing Note) on the site, go to Insert > Drive and select the file. Make sure the file's sharing settings are set to "Anyone with the link can view."
Using Layouts: Use the "Content Blocks" on the right sidebar to make your site look professional quickly.
Publishing: Your site is not live until you hit Publish. You must Re-Publish every time you make a big change!
⚖️ Evaluation Criteria (What I'm Looking For)
Collaboration: Does the version history show everyone contributed? Are bylines clear?
Evidence: Is your "Evaluating Canada" section backed by a real fact or law?
Connection: Is the link between the Nova Scotia/Canada issue and the UN Global Goal clearly explained?
Citizenship: Does your "Call to Action" actually propose a way to make change?
